WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.601
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-winning actress,

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.067
producer, and director.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:07.133
Her new series, "The Gentle Art
of Swedish Death Cleaning,"

00:00:07.133 --> 00:00:08.901
is available
to stream on Peacock,

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:11.167
and she's kicking off
her Restless Leg Tour

00:00:11.167 --> 00:00:13.567
alongside Tina Fey this weekend
in Washington, D.C.

00:00:13.567 --> 00:00:15.033
Please welcome back
to the show the one, the only,

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.534
the best in the biz, our good
friend Amy Poehler, everybody.

00:00:17.534 --> 00:00:19.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:29.534
♪♪

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:39.534
♪♪

00:00:39.534 --> 00:00:40.534
♪♪

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:43.200
Welcome back.
-Hey, Seth!

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.534
What a crowd!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:48.100
-Look at this.
Look at the love.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:50.367
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Thank you.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:52.734
Thank you very much.

00:00:52.734 --> 00:00:55.734
-Now, I do not see you
barely enough anymore.

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:57.367
-I know.
-But this has been a hot week

00:00:57.367 --> 00:00:59.667
because this is the second time
I've seen you this week.

00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:01.634
-Yeah.
-'Cause you are prepping

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:03.434
for the Restless Leg Tour.
-Right.

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:04.567
So we're getting ready
for our show.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.734
So you very kindly allowed me

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.667
to come and practice
some stand-up here.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.834
-Yes. So we finished
taping on Tuesday,

00:01:10.834 --> 00:01:12.200
and then I got
to tell the audience,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.133
"Amy Poehler is gonna come out
and try stand-up jokes.

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:17.734
-Yeah. And it was a hot crowd,
and I'm very nervous.

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:19.601
I think it gave me
the confidence I didn't need.

00:01:19.601 --> 00:01:20.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:23.167
But, yeah, we're getting
ready to go out,

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.701
and it was really fun
to practice it.

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:27.234
And, you know, I don't want to
blow my stand-up on this show.

00:01:27.234 --> 00:01:28.667
-Yeah, of course.
-I want to get paid for it.

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.067
-You have always said
your art is to get paid for.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:34.868
-I'm always like,
"My C material

00:01:34.868 --> 00:01:36.100
is what I'm going
to bring to this desk."

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.033
-Gotcha.
So if you're watching this,

00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:39.367
if people at home
are watching this

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:40.734
and they don't like the jokes,

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.133
just know this is not
your best stuff.

00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.834
-I didn't even work on it.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.834 --> 00:01:47.000
Literally.
I think before, it was like,

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.133
"What do you and Seth
want to talk about?"

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:50.701
I was like, "Whatever."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:53.334
But -- no, but I did talk
about a couple of things,

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:54.868
and one of the things is that --

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.467
how much I don't
like being pranked.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:57.501
-You have never let --

00:01:57.501 --> 00:01:59.634
And I believe
that people might think,

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.801
"Oh, Amy's so fun.
She's an improviser.

00:02:02.801 --> 00:02:05.267
She loves being on her --
on her toes."

00:02:05.267 --> 00:02:07.200
You hate a prank.
You always have.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.901
-To me, if you throw me
a surprise party.

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:11.133
You're not my friend.
Yeah.

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:12.167
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:14.200
And I would just
turn around and walk out,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.200
and I'd say, "Lose my number."

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.234
I'd scream, "Lose my number!"
to all my friends.

00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:20.601
And, you know, I think
sometimes people are like,

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:22.334
"I don't like pranks,"
but they love pranks.

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:23.501
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:25.100
Well, the way you said it,
really made...

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.501
-"I don't like -- I don't --
Don't prank me, don't prank me!"

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:30.801
I really, really
don't like pranks.

00:02:30.801 --> 00:02:33.801
-Yeah, you remember --
It wasn't a prank, but we --

00:02:33.801 --> 00:02:35.934
When our dear friend,
producer of the show,

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.033
Mike Shoemaker,
when he left "SNL,"

00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.767
we did a surprise party for him.

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:41.267
And you and I
were working together.

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.868
And this is how bad
you are at pranks, as well.

00:02:43.868 --> 00:02:46.133
-Yeah.
-You tried to text me,

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:47.234
"Do you think Shoemaker knows

00:02:47.234 --> 00:02:48.300
we're throwing
a surprise party?"

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.167
And who did you text instead?

00:02:50.167 --> 00:02:51.300
-Shoemaker.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:52.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:55.801
So I think you internally
don't like pranks so much

00:02:55.801 --> 00:02:57.968
that you want to, like,
ruin them for other people.

00:02:57.968 --> 00:02:59.234
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:59.234 --> 00:03:03.734
-I always knew that Shoemaker
would want to know

00:03:03.734 --> 00:03:06.467
that there was a surprise party
being thrown, you know?

00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:09.234
And he's always
very nicely lied and said,

00:03:09.234 --> 00:03:10.667
"I don't even know
what that meant."

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:14.267
And I was like, "It meant we
were having a surprise party,

00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.534
and I blew it."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:17.467
Ugh!
That was a terrible moment.

00:03:17.467 --> 00:03:20.834
-Now, do you -- do you
consider magic in the same --

00:03:20.834 --> 00:03:22.167
-Yeah, I don't like magicians.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:25.000
I don't like magic.
I don't like anyone...

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.667
I don't -- I don't --

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:28.033
I don't [bleep] with magic.

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.000
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.167
But I don't like, "Pick a card."
You know, "Pick a card."

00:03:34.167 --> 00:03:37.300
"No, pick someone else."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:40.100
"Where did the card go?"

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:41.801
I went into my car,
and I left

00:03:41.801 --> 00:03:44.400
because I hate -- I hate --
I don't like magicians.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.534
But I do think
that I was hypnotized

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:49.601
without my consent
by a mentalist.

00:03:49.601 --> 00:03:51.200
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:52.667
I've never told this story.
-Okay.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:55.334
-Because currently
there's a lawsuit pending.

00:03:55.334 --> 00:03:56.667
I'm just kidding.
But I was --

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.534
[ Laughter ]
I was in London.

00:03:58.534 --> 00:04:02.200
Yes, I go to London
and travel.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.000
You're not the only one.
And I was in --

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.267
-That's so weird, though,
'cause the other day,

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:06.901
you were like,
"I don't like going to London."

00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:08.334
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:09.968
-So I was in the U.K.,

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.200
and I was at a fancy dinner
many years ago.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.267
Tons of fancy people there,
expensive dinner, you know.

00:04:16.267 --> 00:04:17.734
-Oh, so at a proper restaurant.

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:19.634
That's what
they say over there, "proper."

00:04:19.634 --> 00:04:21.033
-Oh, they do?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:23.033
That's --
That's quite funny, Seth.

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:24.801
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.801 --> 00:04:27.467
And -- But, uh...

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.367
I sit next to someone

00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:31.000
who is considered,
like, a mentalist.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.567
-Okay.
-Like, uh, you know,

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:35.367
one of those weirdo freaks.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:04:35.367 --> 00:04:37.267
And if I had known that --
-That's a magician who's, like,

00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:41.267
bad at shuffling, right?
-Yeah. No card skills.

00:04:41.267 --> 00:04:42.968
But if I had known
he was like that,

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.200
I would have never
sat next to him.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:47.167
I would have got a taxi or a...
-Black cab?

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:49.601
-Black cab.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:49.601 --> 00:04:52.033
But at the end of the dinner,

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:54.000
I stood up, and I was like,
"Dinner's on me,"

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.501
and I remember everyone
being like, "Really?

00:04:55.501 --> 00:04:58.801
This is going to be like
£50,000 or whatever,"

00:04:58.801 --> 00:05:00.801
because they don't go
by dollars there.

00:05:00.801 --> 00:05:02.434
And, um...
[ Laughter ]

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.434
I don't know if you know.
And --

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:08.467
And I picked up the check,
and I went home, and I thought,

00:05:08.467 --> 00:05:10.734
"I think I was hypnotized."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.734 --> 00:05:12.300
I'm not kidding.
Have I ever told you this story?

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.834
-No.
-I think that he was saying,

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:18.300
like, "You should check
on your food

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.801
and make sure it's picked up."

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:24.300
Like, I think he touched me
in some weird way

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.067
and he made me
pick up the check.

00:05:26.067 --> 00:05:29.000
-You think he mentalized you?
-Or...

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:30.767 --> 00:05:32.734
Or I was just trying
to big-time it.

00:05:32.734 --> 00:05:35.267
-Now, I will say I --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:36.968
Because I have known you
to be a big-timer

00:05:36.968 --> 00:05:39.000
at fancy dinners.
-Yeah.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.300
-And it's one of
the great things about --

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.234
There's one downside to it.

00:05:42.234 --> 00:05:44.100
It's great to be your friend
because the amount of times

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.334
you will stand up and offer
to pay for dinner is the best.

00:05:47.334 --> 00:05:49.300
-Yeah.
-The bad side is how often

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.901
you then realize
you don't have your wallet.

00:05:50.901 --> 00:05:52.968
-That's right.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:56.334
That's right. At least --
At least 4 or 5 times.

00:05:56.334 --> 00:05:59.200
I have made a big --
-Big show.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.667
-A big show.
-Like, you wanted to get up.

00:06:01.667 --> 00:06:03.567
So you wanted to make sure
the rest of the table

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:06.133
didn't hear it by word of mouth.
You wanted everyone to hear.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:07.901
-That's right.
-"Poehler's got it."

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.300
-"I got it, guys,"
and people would be like,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.767
"Come on, no way,"
and I'd be like,

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:12.834
"Ye-- Uh --
Let me take care of this."

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:14.400
-You're like,
"You all work hard."

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:17.834
-Exactly. And then I'd say,
"Oh, I don't have my wallet."

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:19.167
[ Laughter ]

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:20.434
And then everyone will go,
"Okay.

00:06:20.434 --> 00:06:21.901
I guess we'll pay for it then."

00:06:21.901 --> 00:06:23.567
[ Laughter ]

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:24.968
-Yep. Hey, here's another.

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:27.200
So we presented at the Emmys
this last year.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.067
-Yeah.
-And you have --

00:06:29.067 --> 00:06:31.334
This is how little you
like surprises or pranks.

00:06:31.334 --> 00:06:32.701
-That's right.
-You have a little trick.

00:06:32.701 --> 00:06:34.901
A backstage trick.
Because they give you

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:36.534
the envelope, and then --
-Yeah, I hope I don't

00:06:36.534 --> 00:06:39.200
get in trouble for this because
we've hosted many things.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.000
But I like to look
and see who won.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:45.067
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:45.067 --> 00:06:48.801
-And by the way, I remember,
at the time,

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:50.167
while you were like,
"You're allowed to check,"

00:06:50.167 --> 00:06:52.701
this was me.
-Yeah, you did not like it.

00:06:52.701 --> 00:06:54.234
And I was like, "Who cares?
What are they gonna do?

00:06:54.234 --> 00:06:55.834
Kick us out of here?"
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.501
-I was like --
And I was, like, checking.

00:06:57.501 --> 00:06:58.734
And if you squeeze sometimes,

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:00.567
if you squeeze
the envelope like this,

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:02.767
you can read the name, or you
can hold it up to the light.

00:07:02.767 --> 00:07:04.367
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:06.300
-So I like to do that.
And we gave it to

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.734
the well-deserving, amazing
Sheryl Lee Ralph.

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:11.200
-Yeah, it was awesome.
-She was amazing.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.133
There she is.
-That is so fun.

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:14.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:14.734 --> 00:07:19.100
But what I have found is,
when you know who's getting it,

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:20.968
it's very hard
not to look at them.

00:07:20.968 --> 00:07:22.267
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:22.267 --> 00:07:24.033
-So I was like...

00:07:24.033 --> 00:07:25.501
[ Laughter ]

00:07:25.501 --> 00:07:27.067
"When I found out
it was Sheryl Lee Ralph,

00:07:27.067 --> 00:07:28.601
I was just staring
at Sheryl Lee Ralph like...

00:07:28.601 --> 00:07:30.467
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:32.234
"You got this.
You're gonna win."

00:07:32.234 --> 00:07:34.267
[ Laughter ]
I was like --

00:07:34.267 --> 00:07:36.167
I was like, "Get up!"

00:07:36.167 --> 00:07:37.734
[ Laughter ]
So I was like,

00:07:37.734 --> 00:07:39.467
"Ladies and gentlemen,
the winner is..."

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:41.601
[ Laughter ]

00:07:41.601 --> 00:07:43.601
-Now every time
you present in the future,

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:44.934
people are gonna know this,
and they're gonna --

00:07:44.934 --> 00:07:46.567
when you walk up, they're like,
"She already knows."

00:07:46.567 --> 00:07:47.968
-Yeah. Or --
-People are gonna be

00:07:47.968 --> 00:07:50.367
in the audience trying to make
eye contact with you.

00:07:50.367 --> 00:07:52.868
-But I do like -- I do like
knowing who's going to win.

00:07:52.868 --> 00:07:55.868
And I just like knowing just
one second before everyone else.

00:07:55.868 --> 00:07:57.100
-Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:00.334
-[ Laughing ]
-Is that bad? Is that weird?

00:08:00.334 --> 00:08:02.100
-No, I think it's good.
-I mean, I like --

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.234
What made me happy about it was,
when --

00:08:05.234 --> 00:08:08.267
Even though you know
there was one of six

00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:10.901
and you know all their names,
I'm so nervous

00:08:10.901 --> 00:08:12.400
that you're gonna screw up
the moment.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.901
So I like just like having
my lines a little bit earlier.

00:08:14.901 --> 00:08:16.634
-Exactly right.
Exactly right.

00:08:16.634 --> 00:08:17.901
And, you know,
you can change it.

00:08:17.901 --> 00:08:19.200
If you don't like the winner,
just change it.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:20.601
-That's the other rule
they don't know you can do.

00:08:20.601 --> 00:08:22.267
[ Laughter ]

00:08:22.267 --> 00:08:23.834
I remember. Remember,
they gave us the envelope,

00:08:23.834 --> 00:08:26.067
they go, "No peeking.
And also, say whoever you want."

00:08:26.067 --> 00:08:28.667
[ Laughter ]

00:08:28.667 --> 00:08:30.567
I want to ask about
your wonderful new show.

00:08:30.567 --> 00:08:32.968
We'll be right back with more
from Amy Poehler.

